## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

### Pattern Classification

| Original Code | Pattern | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to exit the loop naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes to Supporting Methods

```csharp
// BEFORE: GetNextItem() may block indefinitely (e.g., BlockingCollection.Take())
private WorkItem GetNextItem() { ... }

// AFTER: Accept token so blocking calls can be interrupted
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Example: BlockingCollection supports cancellation natively
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
}
```

If `GetNextItem` uses `Thread.Sleep` or `WaitHandle.WaitOne`:
```csharp
// Replace Thread.Sleep(ms) with:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms); // returns immediately when cancelled

// Replace event.WaitOne() with:
WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { event, token.WaitHandle });
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---|---|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `Stop()` signals cancellation but the thread stays stuck in `GetNextItem()` forever |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Timer and callback leaks accumulate |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions |
| **Expecting instant stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
| **Removing cleanup logic from the catch block** | The `ThreadAbortException` catch had `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` — this must be preserved in the new `OperationCanceledException` catch |

### Thread.Join vs. the Old Sleep+Abort

The original code waited 2 seconds then force-killed. The replacement uses `Join(timeout)` which blocks until the thread exits *or* the timeout elapses. If the thread doesn't exit, it's still running — but in .NET 8 you **cannot force-kill it**. The only remedy is ensuring your code cooperates with the token at every blocking point.
